#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Write the talks of each monthly meetup into the page of that meetup.

bin/import-meetups wrote 266 of the 268 pages of content/events/meetups/
out of the Meetup export: the day, the venue, the group, and the
registrations. The other two are the call for papers mentoring sessions,
which that import skips with the large editions and which were written by
hand.
The talks were not in it, because the export announces them in prose
written by a different volunteer over 19 years and no single parser reads
that. A model reads each description instead, and bin/check-extraction
compares every title and every description it returns against the
normalized source, field by field. This script is the last step: it puts a
checked extraction into the pages.

Run the check first. This script does not re-run it, and it cannot: the
check needs the source events, which are in the export and not in this
repository. An extraction that fails the check must not be imported.

    bin/check-extraction out-a.json a-2021-2026.json
    bin/import-talks out-a.json out-b.json out-c.json --out content/events/meetups

The talks block it writes, and why each field is in it or not:

  id              slugify() of the title, the same rule that makes the file
                  name of a meetup, because this id is an address too:
                  content/talks/_content.gotmpl builds one page per talk at
                  /talks/<event file>/<talk id>/. Unique inside its own file,
                  which is the scope the adapter and a photo reference need.
                  Two talks of one meetup with the same title get -2 and -3,
                  in the order the extraction lists them, so the id of a talk
                  does not move when the file is written again.
  title           copied, through canon()
  speakers        the ids of the content/people/ records the announced names
                  resolve to, which is the field partials/agenda_entry.html
                  reads and bin/check-content validates. A name resolves
                  only to a record whose own name is the same text ignoring
                  accents, case, and the punctuation slugify() drops. That
                  is the comparison the 132 names of the thirteen large
                  editions were resolved with, and it is the whole rule:
                  this script never matches a surname alone, never matches a
                  shorter name against a longer one, and never matches a
                  single word, because AGENTS.md forbids merging two people
                  on a first name and the existing ids hold bare first
                  names. A record with draft: true is not a person and is
                  never matched.
  speaker_names   the names the announcement wrote that resolve to no
                  record, and only those. The field is where a name waits:
                  no template reads it, so the page credits nobody, which is
                  what AGENTS.md requires until a human verifies the person
                  and a record exists. A name in speakers is not repeated
                  here, so the two fields never hold the same person twice.
  type            the controlled vocabulary of partials/event_types.html
  language        en, es or ca, and a list of them in that order for a
                  session given in two, which is what AGENTS.md requires.
                  The extraction writes the name of the language, because
                  that is what its source says; the code is this repository's
                  form, so the map below is here and not in the model output.
  duration_minutes  an integer, left out when the extraction has none
  description     copied, through canon(), left out when there is none

  links           the addresses of the talk itself, in the form the thirteen
                  large editions use. The extraction holds 115 links and 63
                  of them are written. See LINKS below for what each of the
                  other 52 is and why it stays out.
  title_lang      NOT written. It is the language of the title, which is not
                  the language of the talk (see partials/title_lang.html),
                  and the extraction does not carry it. A Spanish title with
                  no title_lang is read by a screen reader with an English
                  voice, so this is a gap and not a finished job.

Every string this script writes goes through canon(), the one
normalization of imported text in this repository. The extraction is
already normalized, so this is a second application of an idempotent
function, and it is here so that the rule holds whatever the input did.

Writing a page again replaces the whole talks block, and with it
everything that follows the block in the front matter: rewrite() cuts the
front matter at the "talks:" key and writes the block at the end, so the
session_videos block bin/import-videos puts after it goes too. Nothing
before the block is touched, and a file whose meetup announced no talk gets
no block at all and no empty list, but everything another tool or a person
added inside or after the block is lost, because the extraction does not
carry it. This script is the first writer of the block and it is not safe
to run after the others.

Measured on 2026-10-13, in the tree with 89 channel videos, a re-run over
content/events/meetups/ with the three current extractions changes 77
files and would:

  delete all 65 video links of the talks, the whole output of
  bin/import-videos for the monthly meetups, because a link of type video
  is not in the extraction, and the 3 session_videos blocks after them.

  put 51 names back in speaker_names, 30 of them distinct, that are
  credited in speakers today. They were resolved by hand, against a
  person record whose name is spelled longer than the meetup announced
  ("Ramon Navarro" against ramon-navarro-bosch), which is a judgement
  this script will not make.

So the order is bin/import-meetups, then this script, then
bin/import-videos, and a re-run of this script means running
bin/import-videos again after it and redoing the hand resolutions. Check
a re-run with --dry-run and a diff against a copy of the directory, never
against content/ itself.

Exit codes:
    0  every page was written
    1  an event of the extraction has no page, or a page could not be read
    2  the script could not run: bad arguments, or a missing file

Usage:
    bin/import-talks EXTRACTION.json [EXTRACTION.json ...] --out DIR
    bin/import-talks EXTRACTION.json --out DIR --people DIR --dry-run
"""

import argparse
import json
import pathlib
import re
import sys
import unicodedata

import yaml

sys.path.insert(0, str(pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent))

from canon import canon
from slug import slugify

# The language name the extraction writes, and the code this site stores.
# AGENTS.md: language takes en, es or ca, and a list is written in that
# order, because partials/agenda_entry.html prints the codes in the order
# the list gives them and two orders print the same pair two ways.
LANGUAGES = {
    "English": ["en"],
    "Spanish": ["es"],
    "Catalan": ["ca"],
    "English/Spanish": ["en", "es"],
    "English/Catalan": ["en", "ca"],
}

# The id of the meetup inside meetup_url, which is the only field of the
# page that carries it. The comment at the top of the file names the export
# record too, but a comment is not a field.
MEETUP_ID = re.compile(r"/events/(\d+)/?")

# The default location of the person records, so the usual run needs no
# extra argument. --people overrides it.
PEOPLE_DIR = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "content" / "people"

# LINKS. The extraction holds 115 links. 63 are written and 52 are not, and
# each of the three groups that stay out is a decision and not an accident.
#
# 44 are type speaker_profile: the social account of the person who gave
# the talk, read out of an event description written years ago. AGENTS.md
# forbids rendering those, in the clearest rule it has, so none of them is
# written. Two of the 44 are not even addresses ("twitter.com/mrswats",
# "x.com@pol_avec"), which is a second reason for the same answer.
#
# 4 are a social account the extraction labelled site or github instead of
# speaker_profile. The label is wrong and the content is the same thing: a
# GitHub profile is a path of one segment, github.com/<user>, and not a
# repository, and a medium.com/@handle is a handle. The rule follows the
# content, so these are listed by hand below and stay out with the other
# 44. The personal web site of a speaker, which the label site also brought
# in twice, is not a handle and is written: it is an address of the talk's
# own material in the same sentence of the source.
SOCIAL_PROFILES = frozenset([
    "https://github.com/vperezb",
    "https://github.com/SaxMan96/",
    "https://github.com/oscarbc96",
    "https://medium.com/@victor.perez.berruezo",
])

# 27 of the remaining 71 are http://, which bin/check-rendered rejects on a
# built page, and rightly: an http:// link on an https:// page is a
# downgrade the reader does not choose. They are 26 distinct addresses, and
# each was requested over https on 2026-10-13 with curl -L. 22 answered and
# are written with the https scheme and the same host and path, which is
# the whole change: no redirect target is followed into the stored value,
# because where a site moved to in 2026 is not what the announcement said.
# The four that answered nothing over https are dropped, and they are named
# below so that the next run does not have to find out again.
#
# One of the 22, the afitnerd.com kata post, answers 404 over https and
# over http alike: the host is there and the page is gone. It meets the
# test this table applies, so it is written, and it is the one link here
# worth a second look by a human.
HTTPS_VERIFIED = frozenset([
    "http://aws.amazon.com/",
    "http://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/",
    "http://aws.amazon.com/ec2/",
    "http://afitnerd.com/2012/11/10/change-kata/",
    "http://agilekatas.co.uk/katas/Tamagotchi-Kata",
    "http://boto.readthedocs.org/en/latest/",
    "http://buildbot.net",
    "http://chalice.readthedocs.io",
    "http://conda.pydata.org",
    "http://docs.python.org/library/re.html",
    "http://guillotina.readthedocs.io/en/latest/",
    "http://hadoop.apache.org/",
    "http://httptoolkit.tech/",
    "http://legacy.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0020/",
    "http://pandas.pydata.org/",
    "http://vagrantup.com/",
    "http://webpy.org/",
    "http://www.ansible.com",
    "http://www.fabfile.org/",
    "http://www.froscon.de/",
    "http://www.mongodb.org/",
    "http://www.nltk.org/",
])

# The four http:// addresses with no https at all, and what the request
# said. A host that fails the TLS handshake or never answers on 443 cannot
# be rewritten: the link would be dead, and a dead link is worse than a
# missing one.
HTTPS_NO_ANSWER = {
    "http://codekata.com/kata/kata06-anagrams/": "connection reset on 443",
    "http://flask.pocoo.org/": "certificate does not cover the host",
    "http://iamnotmyself.com/2011/02/13/refactor-this-the-gilded-rose-kata/":
        "no answer on 443",
    "http://python3porting.com/": "nothing listening on 443",
}

# The comment that says where the block comes from, as every other block of
# these files does. %s is the Meetup event id.
BLOCK_COMMENT = """\
# The talks this meetup announced, read from the description of event %s in
# the pybcn-archive export by a model and compared field by field against
# that description by bin/check-extraction. bin/import-talks writes this
# block: a correction goes to the extraction or to the script, never here.
# speakers holds the content/people/ record the announced name resolves to,
# by the same name, ignoring accents and case. speaker_names holds a name
# that resolves to no record: no template reads it, so the page credits
# nobody until a human verifies the person (AGENTS.md)."""


def yaml_string(value):
    """Return value as a double quoted YAML scalar, on one line.

    The same escaping bin/import-meetups writes, plus the newline: a talk
    description holds paragraphs, and the published programmes write those
    as \\n inside a one line scalar. Only the backslash, the double quote,
    the newline, and the tab need escaping inside double quotes.
    """
    body = (value.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace('"', '\\"')
                 .replace("\n", "\\n").replace("\t", "\\t"))
    return '"%s"' % body


def fold(name):
    """Return name with its accents, case, and punctuation set aside.

    slugify() already strips the accents, lowercases, and turns everything
    that is not a letter or a digit into a hyphen, which is exactly the
    comparison the thirteen large editions resolved their 132 names with:
    "Carles Barrobes" and "Carles Barrobés" are one person, and so are
    "Laura Perez-Mayos" and "Laura Pérez Mayos", because a hyphen and a
    space are the same separator in a name written by two volunteers.

    It is not a latinizing transform of stored text: nothing this returns
    is ever written to a page. canon() is the rule for what is stored, and
    it never touches a letter. This function only compares.
    """
    return slugify(unicodedata.normalize("NFC", name))


def person_index(people_dir):
    """Return the id of each person record, keyed by the folded name.

    Keyed by the folded id as well, because an id is the folded name of the
    person in every record of this repository and a source that writes the
    name with a middle name dropped still has to miss, not guess.

    A record with draft: true is left out: content/people/canary.md is a
    fixture for bin/check-rendered and not a person, and crediting a talk
    to it would publish the fixture.

    A key two records share is dropped from the index and reported, because
    a name that matches two people resolves to neither. That cannot happen
    today, and the day a second Jordi Mas gets a record it must fail here
    and not pick one of them.
    """
    if not people_dir.is_dir():
        sys.exit("import-talks: no %s" % people_dir)
    index, clashes = {}, {}
    for path in sorted(people_dir.glob("*.md")):
        if path.name == "_index.md":
            continue
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        found = re.match(r"^---\n(.*?)\n---", text, re.S)
        if not found:
            sys.exit("import-talks: %s has no front matter" % path)
        data = yaml.safe_load(found.group(1)) or {}
        if data.get("draft"):
            continue
        person_id, name = data.get("id"), data.get("name")
        if not person_id or not name:
            sys.exit("import-talks: %s has no id or no name" % path)
        for key in {fold(name), fold(person_id)}:
            if key in index and index[key] != person_id:
                clashes.setdefault(key, {index[key]}).add(person_id)
            index[key] = person_id
    for key, ids in sorted(clashes.items()):
        print("WARN  '%s' is the name of %s, so a source that writes it "
              "resolves to nobody" % (key, " and ".join(sorted(ids))))
        index.pop(key, None)
    return index


def resolve_speakers(names, index):
    """Return (ids, unresolved) for the announced names of one talk.

    A name of one word never resolves. AGENTS.md: never merge two people on
    a first-name match, and the ids of this site include bare first names.
    The source writes "David", "Nuria", "Loris", "Areski", and "Garito",
    and not one of those five says which person it means.

    A name the index does not hold stays in unresolved, in the text the
    announcement used. Order is kept on both lists, and a name that repeats
    inside one talk is written once.
    """
    ids, unresolved = [], []
    for name in names:
        key = fold(name)
        person_id = index.get(key) if key.count("-") else None
        if person_id:
            if person_id not in ids:
                ids.append(person_id)
        elif name not in unresolved:
            unresolved.append(name)
    return ids, unresolved


def talk_links(talk):
    """Return the links of one talk that reach the page, in source order.

    See LINKS at the top of the file for the three groups that do not, and
    for what was measured about each. An http:// address that is in
    HTTPS_VERIFIED is written with the https scheme and nothing else
    changed; one that is in neither table stops the run, because an
    unmeasured address must not be guessed either way.
    """
    kept = []
    for link in talk.get("links") or []:
        url, kind = link.get("url") or "", link.get("type") or ""
        if kind == "speaker_profile" or url in SOCIAL_PROFILES:
            continue
        if url.startswith("http://"):
            if url in HTTPS_NO_ANSWER:
                continue
            if url not in HTTPS_VERIFIED:
                sys.exit("import-talks: %s is an http:// address that neither "
                         "HTTPS_VERIFIED nor HTTPS_NO_ANSWER names. Request "
                         "it over https and put it in one of them." % url)
            url = "https://" + url[len("http://"):]
        elif not url.startswith("https://"):
            sys.exit("import-talks: %r is not an address, and only the two "
                     "speaker_profile entries were. Say what it is." % url)
        if not kind:
            sys.exit("import-talks: %s has no type, and bin/check-content "
                     "rejects a link without one." % url)
        if (kind, url) not in kept:
            kept.append((kind, url))
    return kept


def pages_by_id(out_dir):
    """Return the page of each meetup, keyed by the Meetup event id.

    A directory with no page stops the run before anything is written: an
    id that matches nothing is either the wrong extraction or the wrong
    directory, and both of those have to be seen and not half applied.
    """
    pages = {}
    for path in sorted(out_dir.glob("*.md")):
        if path.name == "_index.md":
            continue
        found = MEETUP_ID.search(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        if not found:
            sys.exit("import-talks: %s carries no meetup_url" % path)
        event_id = found.group(1)
        if event_id in pages:
            sys.exit("import-talks: event %s is on %s and on %s"
                     % (event_id, pages[event_id].name, path.name))
        pages[event_id] = path
    if not pages:
        sys.exit("import-talks: no page in %s" % out_dir)
    return pages


def talk_ids(talks):
    """Return one id per talk, unique inside this page.

    A title that slugifies to nothing, which an all emoji title does, falls
    back to its position, so every talk gets a page. A repeated slug takes
    a counter in the order the extraction lists the talks, so the id is a
    function of the extraction and does not move between two runs.
    """
    ids, used = [], {}
    for position, talk in enumerate(talks, start=1):
        base = slugify(canon(talk.get("title") or "")) or "talk-%d" % position
        used[base] = used.get(base, 0) + 1
        ids.append(base if used[base] == 1 else "%s-%d" % (base, used[base]))
    return ids


def talk_block(event_id, talks, people, tally):
    """Return the talks block of one page, comment included, as lines.

    people is the index of person_index(). tally counts what the run
    resolved, so main() can report it: this is the one place that sees
    every name and every link of every talk.
    """
    lines = BLOCK_COMMENT.split("\n")
    lines = [line % event_id if "%s" in line else line for line in lines]
    lines.append("talks:")
    for talk_id, talk in zip(talk_ids(talks), talks):
        lines.append("  - id: %s" % talk_id)
        lines.append("    title: %s" % yaml_string(canon(talk["title"]).strip()))
        names = [canon(name).strip() for name in talk.get("speakers") or []]
        ids, unresolved = resolve_speakers(names, people)
        tally["names"] += len(names)
        tally["resolved"] += len(names) - len(unresolved)
        tally["unresolved"].update(unresolved)
        tally["credited"].update(ids)
        if ids:
            lines.append("    speakers: [%s]" % ", ".join(ids))
        if unresolved:
            lines.append("    speaker_names: [%s]"
                         % ", ".join(yaml_string(name) for name in unresolved))
        lines.append("    type: %s" % talk["type"])
        language = LANGUAGES.get(talk.get("language"))
        if language:
            lines.append("    language: %s"
                         % (language[0] if len(language) == 1 else
                            "[%s]" % ", ".join(language)))
        elif talk.get("language"):
            sys.exit("import-talks: event %s talk '%s' has language %r, which "
                     "is not one of %s" % (event_id, talk_id, talk["language"],
                                           ", ".join(sorted(LANGUAGES))))
        if talk.get("duration_minutes") is not None:
            lines.append("    duration_minutes: %d" % int(talk["duration_minutes"]))
        description = canon(talk.get("description") or "").strip()
        if description:
            lines.append("    description: %s" % yaml_string(description))
        links = talk_links(talk)
        tally["links"] += len(links)
        if links:
            lines.append("    links:")
            for kind, url in links:
                lines.append("      - type: %s" % kind)
                lines.append("        url: %s" % yaml_string(url))
    return lines


def rewrite(text, block):
    """Return the page text with block as its talks block.

    The block goes at the end of the front matter, which is where the two
    blocks these pages are waiting for belong: the front matter is the page
    (see layouts/_default/meetup.html) and the body is empty on purpose.
    An earlier block of this script, and the comment above it, is replaced,
    so a second run writes the same file and not a second block.
    """
    lines = text.split("\n")
    if lines[0].strip() != "---":
        return None
    try:
        close = lines.index("---", 1)
    except ValueError:
        return None

    head = lines[:close]
    for position, line in enumerate(head):
        if line.rstrip() == "talks:":
            # Back up over the comment that introduces the block, so the
            # comment is replaced with it and does not pile up.
            start = position
            while start and head[start - 1].startswith("#"):
                start -= 1
            head = head[:start]
            break

    return "\n".join(head + list(block) + lines[close:])


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Write the talks of each meetup into the page of that meetup.")
    parser.add_argument("extraction", nargs="+", type=pathlib.Path,
                        help="the checked extraction JSON files")
    parser.add_argument("--out", type=pathlib.Path, required=True,
                        help="the directory of the meetup pages")
    parser.add_argument("--people", type=pathlib.Path, default=PEOPLE_DIR,
                        help="the directory of the person records "
                             "(default: content/people)")
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                        help="report what would be written and write nothing")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    if not args.out.is_dir():
        sys.exit("import-talks: no %s" % args.out)
    for path in args.extraction:
        if not path.is_file():
            sys.exit("import-talks: no %s" % path)

    pages = pages_by_id(args.out)
    entries = []
    for path in args.extraction:
        entries += json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))

    missing = [str(entry.get("event_id")) for entry in entries
               if str(entry.get("event_id")) not in pages]
    if missing:
        print("FAIL  %d event(s) of the extraction have no page in %s: %s"
              % (len(missing), args.out, ", ".join(missing)))
        return 1

    people = person_index(args.people)
    tally = {"names": 0, "resolved": 0, "links": 0,
             "unresolved": set(), "credited": set()}
    written, talks, empty, disambiguated = 0, 0, 0, 0
    for entry in entries:
        event_id = str(entry["event_id"])
        path = pages[event_id]
        found = entry.get("talks") or []
        if not found:
            empty += 1
            continue
        bases = [slugify(canon(talk.get("title") or "")) for talk in found]
        disambiguated += len(bases) - len(set(bases))
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        new = rewrite(text, talk_block(event_id, found, people, tally))
        if new is None:
            print("FAIL  %s has no front matter to write into" % path)
            return 1
        if not args.dry_run and new != text:
            path.write_text(new, encoding="utf-8")
        written += 1
        talks += len(found)

    print("%s %d talks into %d of the %d pages in %s"
          % ("would write" if args.dry_run else "wrote", talks, written,
             len(pages), args.out))
    print("%d events announced no talk and get no talks block" % empty)
    print("%d talk id(s) needed a counter to stay unique inside their page"
          % disambiguated)
    print("%d of %d announced names resolve to %d of the %d person records; "
          "%d names, %d of them distinct, wait in speaker_names"
          % (tally["resolved"], tally["names"], len(tally["credited"]),
             len(set(people.values())),
             tally["names"] - tally["resolved"], len(tally["unresolved"])))
    print("%d links written of the 115 in the extraction" % tally["links"])
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
